From 2cbeefaee6a1978ea4f3d5d91be9326980ba1bea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (draft)" <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 15:50:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] mm/khugepaged: don't run at MAX_NICE

khugepaged runs at MAX_NICE so that it stays out of the way. But while
it collapses a range it holds locks that the scanned process itself
needs:

 - collapse_huge_page(): the mmap_lock for read while revalidating and
   swapping in, then for write together with the VMA write lock and
   the anon_vma lock while it flushes, isolates and copies up to 2 MiB;
 - collapse_file(): the page cache folios of the range, while it reads
   missing pages and copies them into the new folio;
 - collapse_scan_mm_slot(): the mmap_lock for read while scanning;
 - with khugepaged/defrag=1, direct compaction during the allocation:
   the folios it migrates stay locked and unmapped until they are moved.

On a CPU that is busy with nice 0 work - a game, its shader compiler
threads - a MAX_NICE task (weight 15 against 1024) can wait a long time
before it runs again. If that happens while khugepaged holds one of
these locks, or while it is queued for the mmap_lock as a writer (which
already makes new readers wait), every thread of the process that calls
mmap()/munmap()/mprotect(), faults on that VMA, on a mmap_lock fallback
path or on a folio being migrated, or reads that file range, waits for
the lowest priority thread on the system. Wine/Proton and game memory
allocators call mmap()/mprotect() often.

Run khugepaged at the default priority. Scanning and copying are cheap,
and khugepaged sleeps between scans (scan_sleep_millisecs). With
khugepaged/defrag=0 its allocation doesn't reclaim or compact either;
with defrag=1 that work now competes at normal priority, which is the
price for not holding migrating folios at MAX_NICE. Renicing khugepaged
from userspace still works.

DRAFT for linux-lunar; not submitted upstream. Not compiled or run yet.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Jpc2umnveVKWeq6gZjkGU7
---
 mm/khugepaged.c | 17 ++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/mm/khugepaged.c b/mm/khugepaged.c
index f01ac62b7..656ba2de5 100644
--- a/mm/khugepaged.c
+++ b/mm/khugepaged.c
@@ -3041,7 +3041,22 @@ static int khugepaged(void *none)
 	struct mm_slot *slot;
 
 	set_freezable();
-	set_user_nice(current, MAX_NICE);
+	/*
+	 * No set_user_nice(current, MAX_NICE) here: a collapse takes locks
+	 * that the scanned process itself needs. Its mmap_lock (read while
+	 * scanning and swapping in, write while isolating and copying), the
+	 * VMA write lock and the anon_vma lock, or for file and shmem
+	 * collapse the page cache folios of the range. With khugepaged/defrag
+	 * the allocation also compacts, which locks and unmaps the folios it
+	 * migrates. At MAX_NICE on a busy CPU, khugepaged can wait a long time
+	 * to run again while holding any of these, or while queued for the
+	 * mmap_lock as a writer, which already blocks new readers. Every
+	 * thread that calls mmap()/munmap()/mprotect(), faults on that VMA, on
+	 * a mmap_lock fallback path or on a folio being migrated, or reads
+	 * that file range, then waits for the lowest priority thread on the
+	 * system. At the default priority this costs little: khugepaged
+	 * sleeps between scans, and a scan only walks page tables.
+	 */
 
 	while (!kthread_should_stop()) {
 		khugepaged_do_scan(&khugepaged_collapse_control);
